1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,278
A fuel log with a million and a half

2
00:00:03,298 --> 00:00:06,326
rows will not open in a spreadsheet.

3
00:00:06,346 --> 00:00:08,748
Data Noodler is for that job.

4
00:00:08,768 --> 00:00:12,476
It reads big flat files
on a locked-down PC,

5
00:00:12,496 --> 00:00:16,288
answers questions in
plain SQL, and turns the

6
00:00:16,308 --> 00:00:20,440
answer into a dashboard,
all on your own machine.

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,779
The data today is synthetic.

8
00:00:23,049 --> 00:00:25,460
Here is how it fits together.

9
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,994
You double-click Data_Noodler. bat.

10
00:00:28,014 --> 00:00:31,694
That starts a small
PowerShell server that listens

11
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:35,543
on localhost only,
and your browser shows the pages.

12
00:00:36,263 --> 00:00:41,323
Behind it is the data engine, written
in C and compiled in memory at start.

13
00:00:41,343 --> 00:00:45,502
Tables sit in memory as Arrow,
queries spill to disk when

14
00:00:45,522 --> 00:00:49,607
memory runs short, and each
project is a folder on disk.

15
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:53,751
Raw files go in: CSV,
Parquet, Arrow or SQLite,

16
00:00:53,771 --> 00:00:56,883
copied into the project or read in place.

17
00:00:57,603 --> 00:01:02,435
The work in the middle is plain SQL,
saved with the project.

18
00:01:02,455 --> 00:01:05,284
Each query builds a clean table.

19
00:01:05,614 --> 00:01:10,516
Out come dashboards as an image,
PDF, PowerPoint slide or a

20
00:01:10,536 --> 00:01:15,438
one-file web page, and any
table as CSV, Parquet or SQLite.

21
00:01:16,158 --> 00:01:19,244
Semaphore can take that
CSV as a plain file.

22
00:01:19,264 --> 00:01:21,974
Fairlead and Lazarette are not used:

23
00:01:21,994 --> 00:01:25,538
Noodler makes no model
calls and holds no keys.

24
00:01:26,199 --> 00:01:29,865
And the guardrails:
localhost only with a session token,

25
00:01:29,885 --> 00:01:32,235
file access confined to the project,

26
00:01:32,255 --> 00:01:36,053
trash and snapshot archives,
and a label on every project.

27
00:01:36,419 --> 00:01:39,234
Starting it needs no
install and no admin rights.

28
00:01:39,954 --> 00:01:42,861
The control panel shows the web server
and the data engine both online.

29
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:48,328
Every request is listed as it happens,
and Stop Server is one click.

30
00:01:48,348 --> 00:01:52,049
If every window is closed,
it stops itself after two minutes.

31
00:01:52,069 --> 00:01:56,626
Make a project: a name, a description,
and a classification label.

32
00:01:56,646 --> 00:01:59,185
This one is Internal,
and the label travels

33
00:01:59,205 --> 00:02:01,447
with everything the project publishes.

34
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:05,354
Import opens next.
Point it at the CSV on a local drive.

35
00:02:05,374 --> 00:02:06,497
A network path works too.

36
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:09,640
One and a half million rows, a hundred and

37
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:12,097
fourteen megabytes,
counted in under a second.

38
00:02:12,117 --> 00:02:16,721
The columns and their types are
detected, and you can change them.

39
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:20,680
Import writes it as an Arrow
dataset in a couple of seconds,

40
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:22,852
then checks the row count and the column

41
00:02:22,872 --> 00:02:25,296
names against the source
before it says done.

42
00:02:26,027 --> 00:02:27,563
Transform is a SQL editor.

43
00:02:27,583 --> 00:02:31,069
The question: fuel per nautical mile,

44
00:02:31,089 --> 00:02:33,533
by vessel class and month.

45
00:02:34,253 --> 00:02:37,486
The first try fails,
and the error says why:

46
00:02:37,506 --> 00:02:41,774
this dialect has no DATE_TRUNC,
use TRUNC with MM instead.

47
00:02:41,794 --> 00:02:43,674
So we make that change.

48
00:02:44,394 --> 00:02:47,677
Run again. One and a half million
rows grouped in under two seconds,

49
00:02:47,697 --> 00:02:50,785
and a clean table of eighty-eight
rows is saved next to the SQL.

50
00:02:51,055 --> 00:02:56,010
Dashboard Studio. Add a line chart,
open its settings, and click Data.

51
00:02:56,858 --> 00:02:59,417
You build the chart by dragging fields:

52
00:02:59,437 --> 00:03:03,055
month onto the X axis,
litres per nautical mile onto Y.

53
00:03:03,462 --> 00:03:06,515
Then class, to split the lines.

54
00:03:06,535 --> 00:03:10,006
The chart reads the clean
table through the engine.

55
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:13,750
Every class burns more per
mile from December to February.

56
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:18,775
That winter bump is baked into the
synthetic data, and the chart finds it.

57
00:03:18,795 --> 00:03:21,735
Publish writes the dashboard
as a web page and a PDF.

58
00:03:21,755 --> 00:03:24,787
Image and PowerPoint
slide are one tick away.

59
00:03:25,507 --> 00:03:28,027
Any table can be exported too.

60
00:03:28,047 --> 00:03:30,559
Here the clean table goes out as CSV,

61
00:03:30,579 --> 00:03:33,501
ready to hand to
Semaphore as a plain file.

62
00:03:34,221 --> 00:03:37,494
And the web page opens
straight from the file,

63
00:03:37,514 --> 00:03:40,142
with no server and no data behind it.

64
00:03:40,162 --> 00:03:41,860
Its header carries the Internal label.

65
00:03:42,630 --> 00:03:47,649
Next month's log arrives,
and nobody wants to repeat all of this.

66
00:03:47,669 --> 00:03:52,040
Update re-runs the project's
saved setup, in order.

67
00:03:52,714 --> 00:03:56,134
The first run sets the baseline:
import, transform, dashboard,

68
00:03:56,154 --> 00:03:59,242
publish, and a zipped snapshot,
in about twenty seconds.

69
00:03:59,962 --> 00:04:02,548
Now fifty thousand new rows
land in the source file.

70
00:04:03,118 --> 00:04:06,923
Update compares the original file's
size and date with what it last read.

71
00:04:07,643 --> 00:04:10,929
It sees the change, reads the file again,

72
00:04:10,949 --> 00:04:15,848
rebuilds the table, and republishes,
in about eleven seconds.

73
00:04:15,868 --> 00:04:18,947
Ninety-two rows now, up from eighty-eight.

74
00:04:19,217 --> 00:04:21,299
Here is the evidence trail.

75
00:04:21,319 --> 00:04:24,573
The project log lists
every step with a time:

76
00:04:24,593 --> 00:04:27,774
the import, the update,
each file published.

77
00:04:28,563 --> 00:04:30,580
Snapshots are zipped
copies you can restore.

78
00:04:31,289 --> 00:04:33,366
And in this whole
recording the browser made

79
00:04:33,386 --> 00:04:35,320
no request to any host but this computer.

80
00:04:35,898 --> 00:04:39,978
Data Noodler does one job:
it makes a file too big

81
00:04:39,998 --> 00:04:44,161
for a spreadsheet answerable,
on your own computer.

82
00:04:44,181 --> 00:04:47,247
It proposes nothing and sends nothing.

83
00:04:47,267 --> 00:04:52,607
The data was synthetic, and the
timings are from this one machine.

84
00:04:52,627 --> 00:04:56,606
The SQL, the log and the
snapshots are the trail.
